.TH GXWI-FILE-EXPLORER 1 2026-10-19 "Peios gxwi-file-explorer 0.1.0"
.SH NAME
gxwi-file-explorer \- browse and manage files on a GXWI desktop
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gxwi-file-explorer
.RI [ DIRECTORY ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Open a directory in a GXWI session.
With no path, use HOME when available.
The window owns navigation, selection, and sorting; devices displaying the same window see that shared state.
A listing shows at most 2,000 entries and reports when that limit is reached.
.PP
Opening a directory navigates into it; opening an executable starts it.
Other files use an installed application that supports their media type, preferring personal then machine defaults and otherwise the candidates by title.
The entry menu offers alternative candidates.
The listing determines media types by name; unknown types can be checked for text when opened.
.PP
Entry actions include Rename, Copy path, Cut, Copy, Delete, and Permissions.
Delete asks first and removes directories recursively without a trash store.
The directory menu offers Paste, new folders and files, and applications that open directories.
A pasted copy chooses a copy name when needed; a cut does not overwrite an occupied name.
.PP
Cut and copy selections are shared across windows through the session runtime directory.
Without that directory, they remain local to one window.
Paths that are not UTF-8 cannot be placed in this clipboard format.
Concurrent temporary-file collisions do not overwrite unrelated files.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Application declarations and personal or machine file-type defaults are read through GXWI's application catalog.
The session clipboard file retains the name gexora.held for interoperability.
.SH SECURITY
Local actions retain native filename bytes independently of their display labels.
Shared clipboard operations refuse non-UTF-8 paths explicitly.
Unknown-type text detection reads at most 4 KiB from a regular file and does not wait on a pipe or device.
Filesystem operations use the session account.
Permission edits use a retained object handle and the rights available when it was opened.
Delete removes a directory recursively without a trash store.
Copy, move, and rename refuse occupied destinations, including concurrent creation.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The application session ended normally.
.TP
.B 1
A connection, initialization, or application error prevented normal operation.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GXWI_DISPLAY
The display connection supplied by the desktop session.
Launch through the desktop so inherited session descriptors and identity are available.
.TP
.B HOME
The initial or fallback home-directory location.
.TP
.B GXWI_RUNTIME_DIR
Session-private directory containing the shared cut or copy selection in gexora.held.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/apps/dev.peios.gxwi-file-explorer.toml
The application declaration.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gxwi-text-editor (1),
.BR gxwi-terminal (1),
.BR gxwi-sd-editor (1).
